============================== CHANGE 1 replace before[65:67] after[65:67]
  (context before) The half with the most people holds 92.7% of them, and when I computed it for 2020 its centre came out a little west of Berlin.
  - A published map (Bill Rankin's, as the site Brilliant Maps describes it) puts the centre in western Switzerland.
  - The two need not disagree, though the description gives neither the date of that map nor the data behind it.
  + A published map, Bill Rankin's of 2015, puts the centre in western Switzerland, with 92.9% of the people of the year 2000.
  + The two do not disagree.
============================== CHANGE 2 replace before[75:76] after[75:76]
  (context before) <p>The half with the most land and the half with the most people are not the same half.
  - The first holds 88.5% of the world's people.</p>
  + The first holds 88.5% of the world's people; the half centred on Nantes, the usual one, holds 91.1%.</p>
============================== CHANGE 3 replace before[84:85] after[84:85]
  (context before) That is only partly so.
  - What lies opposite South America is the islands from Sumatra to the Philippines, and the best half does leave those out.
  + What lies opposite the tropical part of South America is the islands from Sumatra to the Philippines, with the south of Vietnam and Cambodia, and the best half does leave those out.
============================== CHANGE 4 replace before[106:107] after[106:113]
  (context before) It held on three maps of the coast and in Affholder's work, but every map of a coast is simplified, and a coast is not a fixed line.
  - And the move of the people's centre from the Alps to near Berlin is a statement about one set of population maps, which is itself an estimate of where people live.</p>
  + And the move of the people's centre from the Alps to near Berlin is a statement about one set of population maps, which is itself an estimate of where people live.
  + Every one of its grids, 2020 included, starts from censuses of about 2010 (2005 to 2012 for the countries on the two slivers), carried backward and forward by growth rates and scaled to the United Nations' country totals of 2022; the grids for 2025 and 2030 use the UN's medium projection.</p>
  + <p>Added 26 November.
  + The map of the half with the most people is Bill Rankin's "Human Hemisphere" of 2015 (radicalcartography.net).
  + It gives 92.9% of the people of the year 2000 and a centre in western Switzerland, and warns that the data do not allow the centre to be placed more exactly; for that year my figures agree with his.
  + A search of some thirty-five requests found nobody who had worked it out for a later year or said that the centre has moved, and nobody who had asked the question of woodpeckers.
  + That is what one search found, not proof that nobody has.</p>
============================== CHANGE 5 replace before[111:112] after[117:121]
  (context before) The second did not repeat the searches for the best centres or the checks with other maps of the coast, did not try the globe, and saw nothing of Boggs's, Berge
  - After those readings I folded the two sections on people and on woodpeckers behind their headings, to shorten the page, without changing a word of them.</p>
  + After those readings I folded the two sections on people and on woodpeckers behind their headings, to shorten the page, without changing a word of them.
  + On 26 November a further fresh copy, with the web, searched for earlier work on the people's half and the woodpeckers' half and read Rankin's own pages.
  + Three sentences in those two sections, the last sentence of the cautions and the paragraph dated 26 November are in its words; I checked its quotations against the pages it fetched, and counted again, from the data, the 91.1%, the agreement with Rankin for the year 2000, the places it names and the years of the censuses; nobody has read its sentences since.
  + Its report is among the files (<a href="files/prior-art.html">prior-art.md</a>).</p>
============================== CHANGE 6 insert before[122:122] after[131:134]
  (context before) The story's eleven of seventeen is the nine of thirteen and the two of four counted below, taken together.</p>
  + <p class="small">Added 26 November: two sentences of the older page below are now known to say too much, and they are left as they stood.
  + Of South America's woodpeckers and tropical Asia's being on opposite sides of the globe it says "They are not"; the story above has it right, "only partly so".
  + And where it says the description I read gives neither the map's date nor its data, Rankin's own page gives both: the year 2000, and a population map called GRUMP.</p>
